#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331248 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "624215ff-efdc-5b12-8d61-9d66371672d9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Valid Region to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f86e3899-8e2b-50c3-86c0-66c4ffe8f623" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331250} {:answer/id #uuid "010e222e-228e-58a9-8d7c-9be146dba56f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331251} {:answer/id #uuid "f2513807-eae9-58a0-b677-6a0299fb7a3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331252} {:answer/id #uuid "95d1bcb9-311e-5b4a-9771-f8ab0cb83999" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331253} {:answer/id #uuid "46ee0f1d-445d-53d9-b18f-a023bef243e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95d1bcb9-311e-5b4a-9771-f8ab0cb83999" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331253} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0d50477-0e2e-5729-8b81-5cc6073d4a3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2998acef-0e56-5ee3-8025-29355342aad4" :question/math-academy-id "q-129658" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053940/assets/b946843e712c6294e261cb3015b02bc065cd265763805cf046009a2db0d11b83.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nRequire $D\\subseteq R$, so the rectangle contains the entire region. Which of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\n- $R=[-1,3]\\times [-2,1]$\n- $R=[-1,2]\\times [-2,2]$\n- $R=[-2,3]\\times [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "The displayed region includes points with $y>1$, so rectangle I, $[-1,3]\\times[-2,1]$, does not contain all of $D$. It includes points with $x>2$, so rectangle II, $[-1,2]\\times[-2,2]$, also fails the containment requirement. The entire drawn region lies in rectangle III, $[-2,3]\\times[-2,2]$.\n\nFor rectangle III, $f=e^{x+y}$ on $D$ and zero on $R\\setminus D$, giving $\\iint_R f\\,dA=\\iint_De^{x+y}\\,dA$. Therefore the answer is \"III only\"."}]}
